<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Immae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">272</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Immae was fought in 272 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> between the Roman emperor Aurelian and the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Zabdas</a>. Aurelian's cavalry feigned flight until the heavily armoured Palmyrene horsemen were exhausted, then turned and defeated them. Aurelian then won again at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and captured Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-immae.jpg" alt="Battle of Immae" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original:Demis project Current version:Barosaurus Lentus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AurelianusPalmyra272.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the Crisis of the Third Century Rome lost its ability to defend its eastern provinces against Sassanid invasion. Septimius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odaenathus">Odaenathus</a>, a chieftain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a>, raised an army that repelled the Sassanid onslaught, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallienus">Gallienus</a> made him king and protector of the east. After his death his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> took direct control, through her son, of the provinces under Palmyrene protection, extended her rule into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and won over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. In public she kept up a partnership with Rome, placing her son below Aurelian on documents and coins.</p><p>Aurelian regarded her move into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, a province held personally by the emperor, as a declaration of war, but Germanic invasions kept him busy. Only after defeating the Alamanni, building city walls and abandoning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> did he turn east. He sent a general with a fleet against the Palmyrene garrison of Egypt and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> had her son declared augustus and sent an army under her general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Zabdas</a> to meet him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The armies met at Immae, close to present-day Reyhanli in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, with infantry in the centre and cavalry on the wings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Zabdas</a> relied on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cataphract">cataphracts</a> and on the heat, to which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> were not used. Aurelian adopted a tactic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius Gothicus</a> had used against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>. When Zabdas ordered a cavalry charge, the Roman light cavalry met it, then broke and fled. The heavily armoured Palmyrene horsemen gave chase until men and horses were worn out by the long pursuit in the sun, and at a chosen point the Romans turned and attacked them. Very few of the Palmyrene cavalry returned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>With his cavalry lost, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabdas">Zabdas</a> ordered his infantry back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, and he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zenobia">Zenobia</a> led their resupplied forces out of the city by night to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a>. Abandoned and fearing Aurelian's reputation for savage retribution, the leaders of Antioch opened their gates. Aurelian neither executed them nor let his troops sack the city, but granted a general amnesty. The news spread through the east, and city after city accepted peaceful reincorporation into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Immae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emperor Aurelian defeated Zenobia's armies at <b>Immae</b> and Emesa in 272, besieged Palmyra and captured her as she fled towards Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/palmyrene-empire">Palmyrene Empire</a></li></ul></section>
